{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# The `(inbound, probe)` dispatch chain is a documented subsystem with no live producer\n\n## Status\n\nDISCOVERED while fixing d103-c3f8-2fbc-4c97 / 727f-b351-59ba-4b3b. Not a regression those\nfixes introduced - it predates them and neither changes it. Filed so it is not lost.\n\n## What is unreachable\n\n`differ._compute_mutations_emit_absent_partner_probes` is the only producer of\n`(inbound, probe)` mutations. It iterates `local_state` looking for entries carrying a\nbound `jira_key` field. Its sole production caller, `run_differs`, passes\n`prev_snapshot` - a FETCHED Jira snapshot, whose entries never carry `jira_key`\n(`fetcher.py`, and the contract shape in `_snapshot_schema.py`). The loop therefore never\nemits, and everything downstream of it is dead in production:\n\n* `_run_differs_inbound_probe_dispatch` in `run_differs.py`\n* `inbound_probe.py` (`ProbeResult`, `ProbeBranch`, the router)\n* `backend.probe_remote` / the `SupportsAbsenceProbe` capability\n\nConfirmed empirically: fed a realistic fetched-snapshot pair, `compute_mutations` emits only\n`jira_new`, `unbound_local` and `field_drift`. The only fixture in the repo that supplies a\n`jira_key`-bearing `local_state` is\n`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/diffing/test_differ_emits_probe.py`.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThis is the mechanism ADR 0028 para 2 relies on to tell \"deleted\" from \"aged out of the fetch\nwindow\" - deletion is proven only by a bounded direct GET returning 404. With no live producer,\nthat discrimination is never exercised in production. Ticket b9b8 already reworked the live DC\ncell `test_a_deleted_dc_issue_is_planned_as_an_inbound_probe` for exactly this reason: it\n\"asserted an observable no production path emits\".\n\n## What is NOT the fix\n\nDo NOT re-enable the arm by making `run_differs` pass a `jira_key`-bearing local_state without\nalso removing `reconcile_helpers.drop_snapshot_differ_local_state_emissions` in the SAME change.\nThat suppression exists only because the caller passes the legacy `(prev, curr)` pair; migrating\nthe caller makes the suppressed emissions correct again, and leaving both in place would silently\ndrop real work.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. [x] Establish whether the absence-probe path is still wanted, or whether ADR 0028 para 2's\n   direct-GET confirmation has since been implemented elsewhere and this chain is genuinely dead\n   code to delete. Record the answer with a citation either way.\n   **ANSWERED — DECISION-3B5F-A/B/C.** Implemented elsewhere, twice, both live:\n   `outbound_differ._safe_get_issue` (L13) and `binding_walk.py:187-196` (ADR para 4), called at\n   `run_differs.py:222` / `:257`. Verified live on DC: Row 14 PASSED in harness run 30933704255.\n2. [ ] ~~If wanted: a RED-first test proving `(inbound, probe)` is produced from the real\n   production call path~~ — **MOOT.** The chain is not wanted (see AC1 + the operator ruling below).\n   No producer is to be created.\n3. [ ] If dead: remove the chain, and remove the fixture-only test with it.\n\n## OPERATOR RULING (2026-08-04): once an issue is deleted in Jira, it shouldn't be resurrected.\n\nThis settles the question DECISION-3B5F-C escalated, and it means the ticket's scope is NOT just a\ndeletion — because rebar resurrects TODAY, on a delay, through a path no ticket has examined.\n\n**Measured, not assumed** (real `BindingStore`): 3 consecutive confirmed-404 `note_absent` calls\nretire the binding; `_retire` (`binding_store.py:564-577`) pops the entry from BOTH `bindings` and\n`reverse`; `get_jira_key` then returns `None`, so the local ticket is UNBOUND; and\n`outbound_differ.py:502-513` emits an outbound CREATE for any unbound local ticket. A fresh Jira\nissue therefore replaces the deliberately-deleted one, roughly 3 passes later.\n\nPrior art agrees with the ruling: Unito will not recreate deleted work items, \"assuming that once a\nwork item is deleted, you don't want it back\", explicitly to stop flows re-creating things you wanted\ngone; Kubernetes reconcilers treat a missing object with `IgnoreNotFound` and never resurrect.\n\n**`is_retired` does not prevent this.** It exists (`binding_store.py:612`) and is consulted at\n`outbound_differ.py:590` — but only to keep retired keys OUT of the GET-probe candidate list. It is\nkeyed by `jira_key`, and the unbound-create arm has no retired check at all. The resurrection path is\nunguarded.\n\n## Plan\n\n**A. Tombstone a confirmed-deleted pairing so it is never re-created.**\n1. Add `BindingStore.is_retired_local(local_id) -> bool` — a reverse lookup over the retired entries\n   (`bindings-retired.json` is keyed by `jira_key` and carries `local_id`, so the data is already\n   there; only the accessor is missing). Same duck-typed/fail-open tolerance as\n   `outbound_differ._is_retired`, since the walk is also driven with legacy stub stores.\n2. Guard the unbound-create arm (`outbound_differ.py:510-513`): a local ticket with NO live binding but\n   WITH a retired one was previously paired with a remote that returned a confirmed 404 — skip the\n   create. A ticket that was never bound still creates normally; that distinction is the whole point.\n3. Make it VISIBLE, not silent: emit a bridge alert / sync-log line naming the local id and the retired\n   key, and state the escape hatch (removing the retired entry re-enables creation) so an operator who\n   genuinely wants a fresh issue is not stuck without a documented route.\n\n**B. Delete the dead legacy probe arm** (AC3): the producer\n`differ._compute_mutations_emit_absent_partner_probes`, the dispatch arm\n`_run_differs_inbound_probe_dispatch`, `route_inbound_probe`, and the fixture-only\n`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/diffing/test_differ_emits_probe.py`.\n\n**C. Do NOT restore same-pass re-create.** DECISION-3B5F-B proposed re-homing it into `binding_walk`;\nthe ruling makes that wrong. `_apply_inbound_delete` / `create_after_hard_delete`\n(`apply_inbound.py:175,220`) are left in place but stay unreachable, and the ADR is corrected rather\nthan the code being re-wired to match a stale ADR.\n\n**D. `drop_snapshot_differ_local_state_emissions` STAYS.** The caller is unchanged, so the suppression\nremains exactly as correct as today. (The ticket's \"what is NOT the fix\" trap applies to the WIRE\noption, which is not being taken.)\n\n**E. Correct ADR 0028.** Its Consequences assert c244 re-creates a hard-deleted issue in the same pass.\nThat is false today and, under this ruling, is no longer desired. Replace it with the tombstone\nsemantics, and note that absence-vs-deletion discrimination lives in `outbound_differ` + `binding_walk`.\n\n## Added acceptance criteria\n\n4. [ ] RED-first: a test proving a local ticket whose binding was retired on a confirmed 404 gets NO\n   outbound create — failing before the guard exists, passing after.\n5. [ ] A never-bound local ticket STILL gets its outbound create (the guard must not suppress ordinary\n   creation) — asserted, not assumed.\n6. [ ] The suppression is observable (alert or sync-log), with the escape hatch stated in the message.\n7. [ ] ADR 0028's c244 Consequence is corrected to describe tombstone semantics.\n8. [ ] `drop_snapshot_differ_local_state_emissions` is still present and still consulted."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785869684534130001,"uuid":"9936f209-9502-43a7-aa3e-8bcc50d8a79f"}